        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most water-based deck sealers are touch-dry in a few hours and ready for feet after about a day, while oil-based sealers take most of a day to feel dry and one to two days for foot traffic. Furniture should wait at least a day longer than that. Cool temperatures, high humidity, shade and heavy coats can stretch every number, and the label on your can has the exact times for that formula.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The easiest way to keep the job on schedule is to set it up right: a clean, dry deck, a mild day with no rain in the forecast for at least 48 hours, thin coats applied out of direct midday sun, and enough daylight left for the sealer to set before evening dew. Do that, and the waiting is the easy part.
          </p>
